  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 212 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery suggests that the plume of deeper\nmonsoon moisture is slowly approaching our forecast area tonight,\nwith broad southwesterly flow aloft to the north of a slowly\nweakening ridge. \n\nToday will see more scattered showers and thunderstorms, with a\nbit more coverage compared to yesterday. A slight increase in\nboundary layer moisture along with slightly cooler mid-level\ntemperatures will lead to a modest increase in instability for the\nafternoon, and convective initiation will likely be aided by a\nsagging mid-level front this afternoon and evening. Both the HREF\nand REFS suggest widely scattered thunderstorms developing by the\nearly afternoon, and at least a few showers and storms are likely\nto linger through most of the evening and early overnight hours\n(especially closer to the front).\n\nThe plume of monsoon moisture isn't expected to move much on\nSunday. The lack of synoptic forcing will likely limit storm\ncoverage across the plains, but scattered showers and storms\nare forecast across the high country during the afternoon hours. \n\nA more pronounced shortwave is expected to move through the region\non Monday or Monday night, leading to an increase in storm\ncoverage across most of the state. Recent models are a little too\nslow with this wave for most of our forecast area to benefit from\nthe increased synoptic scale ascent, with better chances for\nprecipitation off to the south and west of our CWA. Still, widely\nscattered showers/storms are forecast again, perhaps lingering\nthrough most of the overnight hours across the plains where\nthe environment would support elevated convection.\n\nMoisture quickly diminishes by Tuesday as the ridge continues to\nweaken and push off to the east. The latter half of the week looks\nrather quiet with the ridge restrengthening over the southeastern\nCONUS, before slowly drifting back towards the region as we\napproach next weekend. High temperatures should approach 90F\nacross the plains most days, with a gradual warming trend likely\nby this time next week.",
